[Music]  hey folks Chris waters here with  GameSpot on the show floor at e3 2014  I'm in the Ubisoft booth about to talk  to mark Thompson the narrative director  of far cry 4 about the game which has  had a fantastic showing so far at e3  mark it's been a big big one for you  guys yeah I think I mean we obviously  weren't a surprise white people were  expecting us we'd already been announced  but I think the fact that we showed up  with so much playable content and people  could get their hands on and fall into  kyrat and start playing around with the  open world has really got people excited  which is cool and I am one of those  people let's talk for a second about  your ambitions for farcry 4 farcry 3 was  a big game with so much to do and so  much it was so good how do you guys what  are you hoping to build how are you  building on that for far cry 4 where do  you want to take this series yeah I mean  so when we when we ship far cry 3  honestly the the open-world and its  appeal was a surprise it caught us by  surprise as it as a development team  because when you develop the game you're  so close to it and you don't often times  you don't realize and uh and the in the  end after we shipped we found out that  players were spending just as much time  if not a little bit more time than in  story missions they were in the  open-world so it was a almost a 50/50  experience but it really wasn't  developed with that kind of focus the  open world kind of crept along quietly  and then surprised us at the end  so for Far Cry 4 it was really more of a  shift in philosophy to be focusing you  know equal efforts on the story and the  missions and developing that open-world  and all of the systems and you know the  players stories that can be generated by  just playing and exploring the  open-world let's talk about that  open-world for a second in kirat it's a  mountainous region so you guys have a  whole different verticality to deal with  you've got a whole different geography  than the tropical islands of Far Cry 3  so what kind of new opportunities does  that bring for you in terms of design as  a designer it's really uh it's really  liberating to have kind of an extra  layer of verticality right so with the  mountains we obviously have incredible  height differences between peaks and  valleys and  gives us new gameplay opportunities so  when we started prototyping the world we  just saw how just how much elevation we  had we quickly realized that not all of  the navigation mechanics from Far Cry 3  we're going to cut it for that  environment so we started to add some  new things so we brought in the  grappling system so now you can you can  climb on grappling points and jump  between grab it so it's very very fluid  and dynamic system it interacts with all  of the other systems from Far Cry 3 so  mid grapple you can jump off open the  wingsuit and then transition to a  parachute and if you can see another  grapple point you can hit that as well  and one seamless transition which is  really cool and we only we have the the  gyrocopter as well which is a new  flyable a new flyable vehicle so you can  i mean it's it's intended that you can  get from low points to high points  quickly but i've been seeing on the show  floor just as many people have been  using it to a fly and rain down death  from above over outposts  I believe that immediately the wingsuit  was one of my favorite things and I'm  really intrigued about this gyrocopter  specifically how you guys showed it off  during the press conference with the  cooperative play having one player  flying it the other player like maybe  riding maybe jumping off and and sort of  causing that that dual mayhem the  cooperative play must just you know take  those sort of emergent players stories  that can happen in an open world and  just multiply them exponentially yeah I  mean that was the whole idea it was just  adding this extra variable it's like  more more controllable chaos and its  really the whole idea behind coop was  that you could call in a friend to your  open world and then you together could  go on your own like mini mini adventure  create your own story attack an outpost  liberate a bell tower go and do some  hunting find some materials and what's  good is when your friend leaves you keep  all of the progression so if you take  over four or five outpost in a session  with a friend when he leaves you still  keep that in your single-player  experience excellent and we are getting  some noise here because people are  talking about Far Cry 4 here this is the  magic of being on the show floor at e3  so uh we're gonna wrap up soon but I  can't let you go here without talking  about another conveyance that you guys  introduced during the demo and that's  the elephants yeah I mean I think the  elephant has been honestly we knew we  had something cool with the elephant but  again it's caught us by surprise just  how excited people are by the prospect  of the elephant  think the fact that it's hard vehicle  part weapon and it's just it's  generating so many more player stories  than we thought it would be it's a it's  great to see just how excited people get  when they get hands-on with that  elephant it must be pretty excited yet  to see people trying it out and using  this stuff in new ways that you guys  didn't even plan for I I'm sure you've  got some more stuff to announce in the  coming months before the game comes out  that we can all look forward to but when  can I actually get my hands in there  grappled to an elephant fly across the  chasm in my wingsuit in Far Cry 4 it's  going to be on November 18th this year  is going to be on a ps3 ps4 Xbox 360  Xbox one and on PC mark thank you so  much for your time here at e3 2014  that's far cry 4 folks one to watch you  and I  - shut up  [Music]  should I stay or should I go  [Music]

